  "prompt": "Find the common rule that maps an input grid to an output grid, given the examples below. Generate a python script that takes a grid as input and transforms it according to the this rule. Anticipate that this program will be applied to several grids, not only the shown test grid.\n\nYour script must define exactly one callable entrypoint:\ndef solve(grid):\nwhere `grid` is passed as a list of lists of integers (list[list[int]]) and the function returns the transformed grid in the same format.\n\nExample 1:\n\nInput:\n2 1 2 2 6 5 5 6 6 5 5 6 2 2 1 2\n1 6 6 1 5 6 5 2 2 5 6 5 1 6 6 1\n2 6 1 6 5 5 5 2 2 5 5 5 6 1 6 2\n2 1 6 6 6 2 2 2 2 2 2 6 6 6 1 2\n6 5 5 6 5 8 5 7 7 5 8 5 6 5 5 6\n5 6 5 2 8 8 5 8 8 3 3 3 3 3 6 5\n5 5 5 2 5 5 5 8 8 3 3 3 3 3 5 5\n6 2 2 2 7 8 8 8 8 3 3 3 3 3 2 6\n6 2 2 2 7 8 8 8 8 3 3 3 3 3 2 6\n5 5 5 2 5 5 5 8 8 3 3 3 3 3 5 5\n5 6 5 2 8 8 5 8 8 5 8 8 2 5 6 5\n6 5 5 6 5 8 5 7 7 5 8 5 6 5 5 6\n2 1 6 6 6 2 2 2 2 2 2 6 6 6 1 2\n2 6 1 6 5 5 5 2 2 5 5 5 6 1 6 2\n1 6 6 1 5 6 5 2 2 5 6 5 1 6 6 1\n2 1 2 2 6 5 5 6 6 5 5 6 2 2 1 2\nOutput:\n5 8 8 2 5\n5 5 5 2 5\n8 8 7 2 2\n8 8 7 2 2\n5 5 5 2 5\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput:\n8 9 9 3 3 3 3 3 2 2 7 7 8 9 9 8\n9 8 9 3 3 3 3 3 2 7 1 7 9 9 8 9\n9 9 8 3 3 3 3 3 7 2 7 2 2 8 9 9\n8 9 2 3 3 3 3 3 1 7 2 2 9 2 9 8\n7 7 2 3 3 3 3 3 7 8 7 2 2 2 7 7\n7 1 7 2 7 2 7 7 7 7 2 7 2 7 1 7\n2 7 2 7 8 7 2 8 8 2 7 8 7 2 7 2\n2 2 7 1 7 7 8 2 2 8 7 7 1 7 2 2\n2 2 7 1 7 7 8 2 2 8 7 7 1 7 2 2\n2 7 2 7 8 7 2 8 8 2 7 8 7 2 7 2\n7 1 7 2 7 2 7 7 7 7 2 7 2 7 1 7\n7 7 2 2 2 7 8 7 7 8 7 2 2 2 7 7\n8 9 2 9 2 2 7 1 1 7 2 2 9 2 9 8\n9 9 8 2 2 7 2 7 7 2 7 2 2 8 9 9\n9 8 9 9 7 1 7 2 2 7 1 7 9 9 8 9\n8 9 9 8 7 7 2 2 2 2 7 7 8 9 9 8\nOutput:\n8 7 7 2 2\n9 7 1 7 2\n2 2 7 2 7\n9 2 2 7 1\n2 2 7 8 7\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput:\n2 2 5 2 9 9 9 3 3 3 3 3 2 5 2 2\n2 5 4 4 9 5 2 3 3 3 3 3 4 4 5 2\n5 4 5 4 9 2 5 3 3 3 3 3 4 5 4 5\n2 4 4 4 5 9 5 3 3 3 3 3 4 4 4 2\n9 9 9 5 9 6 9 3 3 3 3 3 5 9 9 9\n9 5 2 9 6 6 9 9 9 9 6 6 9 2 5 9\n9 2 5 5 9 9 7 9 9 7 9 9 5 5 2 9\n5 9 5 2 9 9 9 6 6 9 9 9 2 5 9 5\n5 9 5 2 9 9 9 6 6 9 9 9 2 5 9 5\n9 2 5 5 9 9 7 9 9 7 9 9 5 5 2 9\n9 5 2 9 6 6 9 9 9 9 6 6 9 2 5 9\n9 9 9 5 9 6 9 9 9 9 6 9 5 9 9 9\n2 4 4 4 5 9 5 2 2 5 9 5 4 4 4 2\n5 4 5 4 9 2 5 5 5 5 2 9 4 5 4 5\n2 5 4 4 9 5 2 9 9 2 5 9 4 4 5 2\n2 2 5 2 9 9 9 5 5 9 9 9 2 5 2 2\nOutput:\n5 5 9 9 9\n9 9 2 5 9\n5 5 5 2 9\n2 2 5 9 5\n9 9 9 6 9\n\nBelow is a test input grid.\n\nInput:\n5 5 2 5 2 5 5 5 5 5 5 2 5 2 5 5\n5 2 2 5 5 5 2 2 2 2 5 5 5 2 2 5\n2 2 5 8 5 2 2 5 5 2 2 5 8 5 2 2\n5 5 8 5 5 2 5 5 5 5 2 5 5 8 5 5\n2 5 5 5 4 6 6 9 3 3 3 3 3 5 5 2\n5 5 2 2 6 6 9 9 3 3 3 3 3 2 5 5\n5 2 2 5 6 9 6 9 3 3 3 3 3 2 2 5\n5 2 5 5 9 9 9 9 3 3 3 3 3 5 2 5\n5 2 5 5 9 9 9 9 3 3 3 3 3 5 2 5\n5 2 2 5 6 9 6 9 9 6 9 6 5 2 2 5\n5 5 2 2 6 6 9 9 9 9 6 6 2 2 5 5\n2 5 5 5 4 6 6 9 9 6 6 4 5 5 5 2\n5 5 8 5 5 2 5 5 5 5 2 5 5 8 5 5\n2 2 5 8 5 2 2 5 5 2 2 5 8 5 2 2\n5 2 2 5 5 5 2 2 2 2 5 5 5 2 2 5\n5 5 2 5 2 5 5 5 5 5 5 2 5 2 5 5\n\nYour final answer should just be the python script itself, no other text or markdown.",
  "raw_response": "def solve(grid):\n    # grid is a 16x16 list of lists of ints\n    \n    # 1. find the 5x5 uniform block (all cells equal)\n    block_pos = None\n    block_color = None\n    for r in range(12):          # 0 .. 11\n        for c in range(12):\n            color = grid[r][c]\n            ok = True\n            for i in range(5):\n                for j in range(5):\n                    if grid[r+i][c+j] != color:\n                        ok = False\n                        break\n                if not ok:\n                    break\n            if ok:\n                block_pos = (r, c)\n                block_color = color\n                break\n        if block_pos:\n            break\n    \n    # If no uniform block is found (should not happen for the test), fall back to a simple heuristic:\n    if block_pos is None:\n        # fallback: choose the central 5x5 sub\u2011grid (rows 5\u20119, cols 5\u20119) after a horizontal flip\n        r, c = 5, 5   # 0\u2011based indices (i.e. rows 6\u201110, cols 6\u201110 in 1\u2011based)\n    else:\n        r, c = block_pos\n    \n    # 2. Determine the background colour\n    bg = grid[r][c] if block_pos else None\n    \n    # 3. generate the three possible mirrored positions of a 5x5 block\n    candidates = []\n    if block_pos:\n        r0, c0 = block_pos\n        # horizontal mirror\n        rh, ch = r0, 11 - c0          # 11 = 15 - 4\n        # vertical mirror\n        rv, cv = 11 - r0, c0\n        # both (rotate 180)\n        rb, cb = 11 - r0, 11 - c0\n        \n        for rr, cc in ((rh, ch), (rv, cv), (rb, cb)):\n            # check whether this candidate contains the background colour\n            has_bg = any(grid[rr+i][cc+j] == bg for i in range(5) for j in range(5))\n            if not has_bg:\n                candidates.append((rr, cc))\n    \n        # select the candidate with smallest row, then column\n        candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1]))\n        r, c = candidates[0]\n    \n    # 4. extract the 5x5 sub\u2011grid and reverse each row (horizontal flip)\n    out = []\n    for i in range(5):\n        row = grid[r+i][c:c+5]\n        out.append(row[::-1])\n    \n    return out",
  "script": "def solve(grid):\n    # grid is a 16x16 list of lists of ints\n    \n    # 1. find the 5x5 uniform block (all cells equal)\n    block_pos = None\n    block_color = None\n    for r in range(12):          # 0 .. 11\n        for c in range(12):\n            color = grid[r][c]\n            ok = True\n            for i in range(5):\n                for j in range(5):\n                    if grid[r+i][c+j] != color:\n                        ok = False\n                        break\n                if not ok:\n                    break\n            if ok:\n                block_pos = (r, c)\n                block_color = color\n                break\n        if block_pos:\n            break\n    \n    # If no uniform block is found (should not happen for the test), fall back to a simple heuristic:\n    if block_pos is None:\n        # fallback: choose the central 5x5 sub\u2011grid (rows 5\u20119, cols 5\u20119) after a horizontal flip\n        r, c = 5, 5   # 0\u2011based indices (i.e. rows 6\u201110, cols 6\u201110 in 1\u2011based)\n    else:\n        r, c = block_pos\n    \n    # 2. Determine the background colour\n    bg = grid[r][c] if block_pos else None\n    \n    # 3. generate the three possible mirrored positions of a 5x5 block\n    candidates = []\n    if block_pos:\n        r0, c0 = block_pos\n        # horizontal mirror\n        rh, ch = r0, 11 - c0          # 11 = 15 - 4\n        # vertical mirror\n        rv, cv = 11 - r0, c0\n        # both (rotate 180)\n        rb, cb = 11 - r0, 11 - c0\n        \n        for rr, cc in ((rh, ch), (rv, cv), (rb, cb)):\n            # check whether this candidate contains the background colour\n            has_bg = any(grid[rr+i][cc+j] == bg for i in range(5) for j in range(5))\n            if not has_bg:\n                candidates.append((rr, cc))\n    \n        # select the candidate with smallest row, then column\n        candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1]))\n        r, c = candidates[0]\n    \n    # 4. extract the 5x5 sub\u2011grid and reverse each row (horizontal flip)\n    out = []\n    for i in range(5):\n        row = grid[r+i][c:c+5]\n        out.append(row[::-1])\n    \n    return out",
